<title>UK: Sullom Voe reopens as fishermen end blockade.</title>
<headline>Sullom Voe reopens as fishermen end blockade.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-05-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Sullom Voe oil terminal reopened on Tuesday afternoon after the port was closed for a day and a half by a blockade by local Shetland fishermen.</p>
<p>&quot;We've finished now. We're steaming back to Lerwick right now,&quot; said a crew member of one of the boats.  </p>
<p>The fourteen fishing boats retreated at around 1545 local (1445 GMT) having occupied the habour of the Brent blend export terminal since 0700 (0600 GMT on Monday.</p>
<p>The blockade prevented a fully-loaded tanker from leaving the port on schedule at 0700 (0600 GMT) on Tuesday. Another tanker was scheduled to berth at 1600 (1500 GMT).</p>
<p>The fishermen were protesting against unpaid compensation claims from the International Oil Pollution Compensation Fund (IOPCF) for damage caused to fishing grounds by the Braer tanker spill in 1993.</p>
<p>--London Newsroom +44 171 542 5387</p>
